    <statement><p>Let <m>A(x) = \int_0^{x} f(t) \,dt</m>, with <m>f(x)</m> as in figure.</p>
<image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__WHFreeman__Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition__5_The_Integral__5-_4_The_Fundamental_Theorem_of_Calculus_Part_II__5-_4-_35-image-1.png" width="41%"/>
<p><m>A(x)</m> has a local minimum on <m>(0,6)</m> at <m>x=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><m>A(x)</m> has a local maximum on <m>(0,6)</m> at <m>x=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2.5</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>6</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert> Solution:</alert> 


The minimum values of <m>A(x)</m> on <m>(0,6)</m> occur where <m>A'(x)=f(x)</m> goes from negative to positive. 
This occurs at one place, where <m>x=2.5</m>. The maximum values of <m>A(x)</m> on <m>(0,6)</m> 
occur where <m>A'(x)=f(x)</m> goes from 
positive to negative. This occurs at one place, where <m>x=6</m>.


</solution>
